## 4. Types of IAM Policies

1. AWS Managed Policy
========================
Created and managed by AWS.
Common permissions provided by AWS.
You can attach them to users, groups, or roles.
You cannot edit the policy permissions.

Example: AdministratorAccess, AmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess

2. Customer Managed Policy
========================
Created and managed by you/customer.
You define the required permissions.
Can be edited, versioned, and reused across multiple users, groups, or roles.

Example: A policy allowing only s3:GetObject on a specific bucket.

3. Inline Policy
========================
Created and embedded directly inside one user, group, or role.
Has a one-to-one relationship with that identity.
Cannot be reused by another identity.

